{
  "_doc": [
    "The canon continent of the Tensura world (Life 2). canon_map.txt holds one character per chunk; this file says what each character is. Lore, terrain and sources per region: design/lore/world.md.",
    "Climate knobs (multi-noise parameters, -1..1; null keeps the vanilla value). With a 'biomes' palette they mostly shape the terrain (erosion) and steer what vanilla still picks (oceans, beaches, rivers, peaks):",
    "  temperature: < -0.45 frozen, -0.45..-0.15 cold, -0.15..0.2 temperate, 0.2..0.55 warm, > 0.55 hot",
    "  vegetation (humidity): < -0.35 arid, ... , > 0.3 lush (forests, jungle)",
    "  erosion: < -0.78 high peaks, -0.78..-0.38 mountains and hills, ... , > 0.55 flat plains",
    "  strength: 0..1, how strongly the knobs win over vanilla's own noise (1 = the whole nation one climate)",
    "biomes: weighted land-biome palette [[id, weight], ...] for ordinary inland terrain, picked in noise-shaped patches. Oceans, beaches, rivers and high peaks keep vanilla's choice unless replace_peaks is true; then peaks come from 'peaks'. Allowed ids: vanilla 1.21.1 and tensura:{ancient_forest,barren_land,desert_of_death,miasmic_plains} (never tensura:underworld_*).",
    "  Palettes also place Tensura structures: goblin villages need forest/birch_forest/taiga/savanna/plains/jungle, lizardman surface villages mangrove_swamp, orc villages desert, dwarf villages meadow/cherry_grove/savanna_plateau/snowy_slopes, labyrinth trees tensura:ancient_forest.",
    "zones (optional, per region): a distance gradient instead of one palette everywhere, so the biome (and with it Tensura's per-biome area magicule) depends on where you stand. Used by J: Veldora's leaked magicule soaks the Jura forest, so newcomers must not be able to stroll to Tempest. Tensura's area magicule per biome: every unlisted biome (forest, birch, plains, flower forest, swamp, peaks ...) is 500 (safe), minecraft:dark_forest +1500 (2000), tensura:ancient_forest +19500 (20000, kills a fresh soul in about a second; verified in the Tensura 2.0.1.5 jar).",
    "  centre: [x, z] world blocks for radius tests (Tempest = 0, 0). interior: cell characters that are not the region's border for depth tests (default \"~\"; J adds W, the Lake Siss marsh, so the lake shore is not an outer edge). fade: every patch shifts all ring tests by a random amount in [-fade, fade] blocks (on top of the border dither), so ring edges are ragged fringes of whole patches.",
    "  rings: tried in listed order, the first test that passes wins, the last ring has no test. Each ring has a name, 'biomes' (like the region's own) and one test: radius (blocks from the centre) or depth (blocks inside the region's border, to the nearest non-region, non-interior cell). The region's plain 'biomes' stays as the fallback if zones are broken (for J: the safe rim list). Peaks keep their own palette.",
    "  J on the 14,000-block map: the border is 650-950 blocks from Tempest on the Blumund (west), Falmuth (north-west) and Dwargon (north) sides and 1,100-3,000 blocks elsewhere, so rings are measured from the border (rim, depth 280 +- 50: 200-350 blocks deep along the whole border, the 'safe outer edge') and from Tempest (core, radius 200 +- 50, about the inner third of the narrowest 650 blocks); the dark-forest middle is everything between: about 190 blocks wide toward Blumund, 290-420 toward Dwargon and Falmuth, 650+ south and east. Life 2 arrives on the rim (design/data/arrival.toml). Check after editing: java -cp mod/build/classes/java/main:<gson jar> com.yasuke.ttl.world.CanonGrid pack/config/ttl/canon_map.txt pack/config/ttl/canon_regions.json <arrival x> <arrival z>. Terrain that is already generated keeps its biomes; only new chunks follow the rings (/ttlmap <x> <z> and /ttlregion <player> name the ring).",
    "The Lake Siss paint pass adds the W marsh ring after the political trace; W intentionally has no flood seeds.",
    "landmarks: named places in reference-map pixel coordinates (the 2800x2050 political fan map, same space as seeds and origin); a tool converts them to world coordinates (about 5.447 blocks per reference pixel at width 14000; world 0,0 = origin).",
    "ring: open sea around the continent (blocks) before the free, non-canon lands; blend: blocks over which the sea fades into them.",
    "seeds and origin are points on the reference map (tools/canon_map.py), only used when the grid is regenerated.",
    "sites: per canon build or landmark (tools never generate them; tests/test_canon_sites.py keeps them in step with the builds): name, for, centre [x, z] world blocks, radius, fade (blocks), terrain, biomes (optional), why. Inside its biome edge (half-way through the fade, wobbled per site so it is organic) a site's own biomes replace the nation palette for land and peaks (a flat site also for water, shores and cave biomes less than 16 blocks below its ground); no biomes = the nation's (Jura's rings stay). terrain: keep (biomes only); flat (the ground is blended to a plain at 'ground' = y of the first air block, default 70, keep it >= 64 so no sea floods it; the column below is pulled solid, so noise caves under the middle fill up); lowland, hills, mountain (erosion 0.5 / erosion -0.1 / continents 0.75, erosion -0.9, ridges 0.62; explicit erosion, continents, ridges override). Listed order: the first biome site wins, later climate sites win where they overlap, the strongest flat site wins. Outside every site nothing changes. Mod: CanonSites, CanonSiteTerrain; /ttlmap x z names the sites at a column.",
    "Changes only affect chunks that are not generated yet.",
    "paint: water and wetlands traced maps miss, in world blocks (tools/canon_map.py applies them after tracing): lake = ellipse of '~' (size in chunks) with an optional ring of a region; river = polyline of '~' `width` chunks wide over land.",
    "width: the continent's width in blocks (owner chose 14000 on 18/11/2026); paint coordinates are written for paint_width and scale with it. World ≈ (reference px − origin) × width / 2570."
  ],
